---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { buildBreadcrumbSchema, buildFAQSchema } from "../../lib/seo";

const pageFaqs = [
  {
    question: "What is CELPIP Speaking Task 5 really testing?",
    answer:
      "Task 5 is testing whether you can compare options, choose the better one, and persuade someone with practical reasons.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need to discuss both options in Task 5?",
    answer:
      "Yes, but not equally. You should mention both options, then explain clearly why one is better for the situation.",
  },
  {
    question: "What makes a CELPIP Task 5 answer weak?",
    answer:
      "Weak answers often describe both choices but never make a clear recommendation, or they choose one option without explaining why it fits better.",
  },
];

const pageStructuredData = [
  buildBreadcrumbSchema(Astro.site, [
    { name: "Home", path: "/" },
    { name: "Blog", path: "/blog/" },
    { name: "CELPIP Speaking Task 5 Comparing Options", path: "/blog/celpip-speaking-task-5-comparing-options/" },
  ]),
  buildFAQSchema(pageFaqs),
];
---

<Layout
  title="CELPIP Speaking Task 5 Comparing Options | Compare Clearly and Persuade"
  description="A clear CELPIP Speaking Task 5 guide with comparison structure, recommendation logic, and practical language for persuading effectively."
  type="article"
  keywords="CELPIP speaking task 5, CELPIP comparing options, CELPIP speaking strategy"
  structuredData={pageStructuredData}
>
  <article class="section guide-article">
    <a class="guide-back-link" href="/blog">Back to blog</a>

    <header class="guide-header">
      <span class="guide-tag">CELPIP speaking guide</span>
      <h1 style="margin: 0;">CELPIP Speaking Task 5 comparing options</h1>
      <div class="guide-meta">
        <span>March 17, 2026</span>
        <span>7 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="guide-lead">
      <p>
        Task 5 is not only about comparison. It is really about decision-making. You need
        to show that you understand both choices, then guide the listener toward the better
        one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Strong answers sound practical. They do not list features randomly. They explain why
        one option fits the situation better.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="guide-card">
      <h2>A strong Task 5 pattern</h2>
      <div class="guide-steps">
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">1</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Introduce the choice</h3>
            <p>State that you have considered both options.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">2</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Compare the options</h3>
            <p>Mention one good point and one weakness for each option if needed.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">3</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Recommend one option</h3>
            <p>Choose clearly and explain why it fits the situation better.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">4</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Close persuasively</h3>
            <p>End with a short line that sounds helpful and confident.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>Useful comparison language</h2>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li><strong>Option A is more practical because...</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Although option B has some benefits, ...</strong></li>
        <li><strong>In this situation, the better choice would be...</strong></li>
        <li><strong>It would save more time / money / effort.</strong></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>What often goes wrong</h2>
      <table class="guide-mistake-table">
        <tr>
          <th>Problem</th>
          <th>Better move</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The speaker describes both options but never chooses one clearly.</td>
          <td>Make the final recommendation obvious.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The comparison is too general.</td>
          <td>Use practical criteria like time, cost, convenience, or suitability.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The answer sounds repetitive.</td>
          <td>Compare first, then recommend, instead of repeating the same benefit.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The advice does not match the person's situation.</td>
          <td>Keep returning to what the listener actually needs.</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>Next step</h2>
      <div class="guide-links">
        <a href="/blog/celpip-speaking-task-7-opinion-strategy">
          <strong>Read the Task 7 opinion guide</strong>
          <span>Compare clear opinion speaking with comparison and persuasion speaking.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/celpip/speaking">
          <strong>Go to the CELPIP speaking hub</strong>
          <span>Find the next speaking task guide or lesson.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/tutoring">
          <strong>Use speaking coaching</strong>
          <span>Practice with direct feedback on fluency, structure, and clarity.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      <div class="guide-faq">
        {pageFaqs.map((item) => (
          <details class="card">
            <summary>{item.question}</summary>
            <p class="muted" style="margin-top: var(--s-2); margin-bottom: 0;">{item.answer}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
